understand where LangGraph ends

105
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:40,810
and Temporal begins. Which
system owns orchestration?

106
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:44,890
Which one owns retries? Which
one owns execution state?

107
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:48,190
And which one determines
the life cycle of an

108
00:06:48,230 --> 00:06:51,850
agent? There are good
answers to those questions.

109
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:56,850
But every answer introduces another
architecture boundary that I

110
00:06:56,890 --> 00:07:00,850
have to own. And that leads
to another consideration.

111
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:06,080
At this point, the comparison
isn't really Dapr versus

112
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:10,820
Temporal versus LangGraph versus
Microsoft Agent Framework.

113
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:15,700
It's closer to Microsoft Agent
Framework plus Durable Task

114
00:07:15,740 --> 00:07:20,240
or Microsoft Agent Framework
plus Temporal or LangGraph plus

115
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:26,260
Temporal or Dapr Agents. And
potentially, other combinations, all

116
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:28,240
of them can be made to work, and that's

117
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:32,490
important. I'm not choosing Dapr
because the alternatives fail

118
00:07:32,530 --> 00:07:37,440
the requirements. I'm choosing between
several architectures that satisfy

119
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:40,880
them. So the question becomes
one of architectural composition.

120
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:46,240
How many runtime concepts
do I want Articulate to

121
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:50,740
depend upon? Where do I
want the boundaries between

122
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:55,760
agents, workflows and distributed
application infrastructure?

123
00:07:56,180 --> 00:08:00,950
And which combination gives me
enough capability without assembling

124
00:08:00,990 --> 00:08:03,820
more platform than I currently needed?

125
00:08:04,180 --> 00:08:07,340
There's another technology decision
sitting underneath all of that

126
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:08,780
that I want to make explicit.

127
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:11,840
Dapr Agents is currently Python based.

128
00:08:12,180 --> 00:08:15,910
Coming from a C-sharp background
that initially makes Microsoft

129
00:08:15,950 --> 00:08:20,650
Agent Framework particularly
attractive. But I don't think choosing

130
00:08:20,690 --> 00:08:24,000
Python for the AI and
reasoning parts of Articulate

131
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:27,740
is actually a compromise.
Python has become a dominant

132
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:31,550
language across machine
learning and much of the wider

133
00:08:31,590 --> 00:08:38,360
AI ecosystem. Models, evaluation
libraries, data science, tooling, agent

134
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:43,120
frameworks and research implementations
frequently appear in Python first.

135
00:08:43,180 --> 00:08:47,790
And Articulate is deliberately an
AI-native architecture experiment.

136
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:51,440
There's value in working
closer to that ecosystem rather

137
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:55,540
than isolating myself from
it simply because C-sharp is

138
00:08:55,580 --> 00:09:00,460
more familiar. That doesn't mean
that Articulate suddenly becomes

139
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:04,900
a Python platform. The architecture
